          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does low iron make people tired?",
          "answer": "Low iron levels can lead to a condition called iron-deficiency anemia. This condition can make individuals feel fatigued and decrease their overall energy levels. When iron is insufficient, the body struggles to produce enough hemoglobin, which is necessary for transporting oxygen in the blood. Without adequate oxygen, the body's cells and muscles do not function efficiently, leading to feelings of tiredness and reduced exercise tolerance.\n\nPeople with low iron often experience gradual fatigue, which can be mistakenly attributed to lifestyle factors until it becomes significant enough to warrant medical attention. This fatigue can be accompanied by other symptoms such as shortness of breath, palpitations, headaches, and dizziness, as the body tries to compensate for the reduced oxygen delivery. This information is based on the general clinical reference on iron-deficiency anemia.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
